<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto of Bamberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1060–<a href="/years/1139">1139</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Apostle of Pomerania, Saint Otto of Bamberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> (c. 1060–1139) was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Bamberg from 1102 and a missionary to the Pomeranians. On two journeys, in 1124–1125 and 1128, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartislaw-i-duke-of-pomerania">Wartislaw I</a>, he converted the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, baptising thousands at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrzyce">Pyrzyce</a> and other towns. He was canonised in 1189 and is honoured as the Apostle of Pomerania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-of-bamberg.jpg" alt="Otto of Bamberg" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pr%C3%BCfening_Klosterkirche_-_Romanische_Fresken_3b_Otto_von_Bamberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> was born in 1060 or 1061 into a noble family with estates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a> and was related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Staufers</a> through his mother. His elder brother inherited their father's property, so Otto prepared for an ecclesiastical career, probably schooled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirsau-abbey">Hirsau Abbey</a> or one of its daughter houses. When the Salian princess Judith of Swabia, sister of Emperor Henry IV, married the Piast duke Władysław I Herman in 1082, he followed her to the Polish court as chaplain. In 1091 he entered the emperor's service, was made chancellor in 1101 and oversaw the building of Speyer Cathedral. Three biographies were written in the decades after his death, by Wolfger of Prüfening, Ebo of Michelsberg and Herbord of Michelsberg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1102 the emperor appointed Otto Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, and he became one of the leading princes of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He founded more than 30 monasteries and hospitals between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, restored Bamberg Cathedral after the fire of 1081 and rebuilt the Monastery of St. Michael, damaged by an earthquake about 1117. Loyal to the imperial court, he was suspended by a papal party at the Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a> in 1118. At the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> in 1121 he helped negotiate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concordat-of-worms">Concordat of Worms</a>, signed in 1122, which settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a>.</p><p>Earlier attempts to convert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, by force under Polish rulers and by the Spanish bishop Bernard travelling alone and poor, had failed. Sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> in 1124, Otto came as a wealthy man with 20 clergy, servants, 60 warriors supplied by Bolesław and gifts, and as papal legate converted many at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrzyce">Pyrzyce</a>, Kamień, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a> and Jomsborg. He is said to have baptised over 22,000 people and founded eleven churches. Some pagan customs returned after he went home in 1125, so he made a second journey in 1128 with the support of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartislaw-i-duke-of-pomerania">Wartislaw I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Otto died on 30 June 1139 and was buried at Michaelsberg Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>. His plan to consecrate a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> was blocked by the bishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, who claimed metropolitan rights; his former companion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalbert-of-pomerania">Adalbert of Pomerania</a> became Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolin">Wolin</a> in 1140. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-iii">Pope Clement III</a> canonised Otto in 1189. He is patron saint of the Archdiocese of Bamberg and co-patron of the Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and the Diocese of Stettin-Kammin, and his feast is kept in Bamberg on 30 September.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto of Bamberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bolesław invited Bishop <b>Otto of Bamberg</b>, who led missions in 1124 and 1128, baptising crowds at Pyrzyce and persuading an assembly of nobles at Usedom to accept Christianity; the lives of Otto written by Ebo and Herbord describe pagan temples at Szczecin and Wolin being destroyed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>For the West Slavs between the Vistula and the Elbe, the chronicles of Thietmar of Merseburg, Adam of Bremen and Helmold, the lives of <b>Otto of Bamberg</b>, Saxo Grammaticus’ Gesta Danorum and the Knýtlinga saga describe temples and cults in detail.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishopric of Pomerania was founded in 1140 at Wolin after the mission of <b>Otto of Bamberg</b>, with Adalbert as first bishop, and moved to Cammin by 1176.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-cammin">Prince-Bishopric of Cammin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mistelbach, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.92,11.52&amp;year=1111" aria-label="Mistelbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4814 427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Otto of Bamberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5264 697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5264 697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.92,11.52&amp;year=1111">What was here in 1111? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
